# Coin Example: Analytical Approach

## Distributions

* Binomial Distribution

::: columns
:::: {.column width=40%}

- @Definition@: **Binomial distribution** models the probability of getting $k$ heads
  out of $n$ tosses given bias $p$
  $$
  \begin{align*}
    & X \sim Binomial(n, p) \\
    & \Pr(k) = \frac{n!}{k! (n - k)!} p^k (1 - p)^{n-k} \\
  \end{align*}
  $$

::::
:::: {.column width=60%}

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L07.1.Binomial_distribution.png)

::: {.center size=tiny}

PDF (function of $k$) varying the parameters $n$, $p$

:::
::::
:::

* Beta Distribution

::: columns
:::: {.column width=40%}

- @Definition@: **Beta distribution** models the probability of an event
  $$
  \begin{align*}
    & X \sim Beta(\alpha, \beta) \\
    & \Pr(\theta)
      = \frac{\Gamma(\alpha + \beta)}{\Gamma(\alpha) \Gamma(\beta)}
      \theta^{\alpha - 1} (1 - \theta)^{\beta - 1} \\
  \end{align*}
  $$

- @Example@: A coin's bias, a click-through rate, a batting average, the fraction of
  a population that will convert

::::
:::: {.column width=60%}

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L07.1.Beta_distribution.png)

::: {.center size=tiny}

PDF varying $\alpha, \beta$

:::
::::
:::

* Beta Distribution: Properties

::: columns
:::: {.column width=45%}

- **Support**: Continuous PDF in [0, 1]

- **Parameters**:
  - $\alpha$: "success" parameter
  - $\beta$: "failure" parameter

- **Shapes**: Adopts several shapes
  - E.g., uniform, increasing, decreasing, Gaussian-like, U-like
  - $\alpha > \beta$: Skews toward 1, higher probability of success
  - $\alpha = \beta$: Symmetric, centered around 0.5

- **Conjugacy**: Beta is the conjugate prior of the binomial distribution

::::
:::: {.column width=52%}

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L07.1.Beta_distribution.png)

::: {.center size=tiny}

PDF varying $\alpha, \beta$

:::

::::
:::

* Conjugate Prior of a Likelihood

- @Definition@: **Conjugate prior** of likelihood $F$ is a _prior_ that, when
  combined with a _likelihood_ $F$, returns a _posterior_ with the same functional
  form as the prior `#cite("bishop2006prml")`{=typst}
  - E.g.,
  ```{=typst}
  #styled-table(
    headers: ("Prior", "Likelihood", "Posterior"),
    rows: (
      ("Beta", "Binomial", "Beta"),
      ("Normal", "Normal", "Normal"),
    ),
  )
  ```

- @Properties@
  - Prior and posterior have the same distribution
  - Posterior has a closed analytical form
    - Update parameters from the prior using data in multiple iterations
  - Ensures tractability of the posterior

## Analytical Solution

* Coin Example: Estimating the Bias

- @Problem@
  - Toss a coin $N$ times
  - Record the number of heads $Y$ and tails $N - Y$

- @Question@: _"How biased is the coin?"_

- @Remark@: There is **true uncertainty**
  - An underlying parameter (the coin bias $\theta$ ) exists, but it is unknown
    - $\theta = 0$: Always tails
    - $\theta = 1$: Always heads
    - $\theta = 0.5$: Half tails, half heads

* Coin Example: Assumptions

- @Assumptions@
  1. Independent Identically Distributed (IID)
     - _Independence_: Coin tosses don't affect each other
     - _Identically distributed_: Coin's bias is constant
  2. Likelihood $Y | \theta$ is a _binomial distribution_
     - I.e., probability of $Y$ heads out of $N$ tosses, given $\theta$
  3. Prior $\theta$ is a _beta distribution_
     - Adopts several shapes
     - Beta is the conjugate prior of the binomial distribution

* Coin Example: Analytical Solution

- @Recap@: The _posterior_ is proportional to _likelihood_ $\times$ _prior_
  $$
  \Pr(\theta | y) \propto \Pr(y | \theta) \Pr(\theta)
  $$

- Substituting _likelihood_ with a Binomial and _prior_ with a Beta:
  $$
  \begin{align*}
  & \Pr(\theta \mid Y)
  \\
  & = \underbrace{
    \frac{N!}{y!(N - y)!} \theta^y (1 - \theta)^{N - y}}_{likelihood}
  \cdot
  \underbrace{
    \frac{\Gamma(\alpha + \beta)}{\Gamma(\alpha) \Gamma(\beta)}
    \theta^{\alpha - 1} (1 - \theta)^{\beta - 1}}_{prior}
  \\
  & \propto
  \underbrace{\theta^y (1 - \theta)^{N - y}}_{likelihood}
  \cdot
  \underbrace{\theta^{\alpha - 1} (1 - \theta)^{\beta - 1}}_{prior}
  \\
  & = \theta^{y + \alpha - 1} (1 - \theta)^{ N - y + \beta - 1}
  \\
  & = Beta \left( \alpha_{prior} + y, \beta_{prior} + N - y \right)
  \\
  \end{align*}
  $$

- @Key idea@: This formula is how the posterior is updated given the data
  - In case of conjugate prior, the Bayesian update becomes an update of parameters,
    without changing the functional form

* Coin Example: Updating the Beta Prior

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}

- @Example@: uniform prior $\alpha = \beta = 1$, and $N = 4$ tosses with $y = 1$
  head
  - The posterior is $Beta(1 + 1, 1 + 4 - 1) = Beta(2, 4)$
  - The posterior mean is $\EE[\theta] = \frac{2}{2 + 4} \approx 0.33$
  - A 94% credible interval is about $[0.03, 0.65]$

- @Remark@: sampling the same model with `PyMC` approximates this exact
  posterior

::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

```graphviz
digraph BetaUpdate {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.5;
    ranksep=0.45;
    rankdir=LR;

    // prior : orange | data : orange | process : blue | posterior : sage green
    node [shape=box,
          style="rounded,filled",
          penwidth=1.8,
          fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=12,
          margin="0.22,0.14",
          height=0.50];

    edge [color="#A3B1C0",
          penwidth=1.3,
          arrowhead=vee,
          arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=10,
          fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    prior [label=<<b>Prior</b><br/>Beta(α, β)>, fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    data  [label=<<b>Data</b><br/>y heads,<br/>N − y tails>, fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    post  [label=<<b>Posterior</b><br/>Beta(α + y,<br/>β + N − y)>, fillcolor="#DFEDE0", color="#8FB79A", fontcolor="#2E5A3D"];

    prior -> post;
    data  -> post;
}
```

::::
:::

* Coin Example: Effect of Priors (1/2)

::: columns
:::: {.column width=40%}

- **Setup**: The true (unknown) value of the coin bias is 0.35

- **Procedure**
  1. Start with 3 different priors
     - \red{Red}: Uniform prior
       - All bias values equally probable
     - \green{Green}: Gaussian-like prior around 0.5
       - Coin mostly unbiased
     - \blue{Blue}: Skewed towards tail
       - Coin biased
  2. Apply data to update the posterior distribution
  3. Update model

::::
:::: {.column width=60%}

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L07.1.Updating_the_prior.png)

::::
:::

* Coin Example: Effect of Priors (2/2)

::: columns
:::: {.column width=45%}

- **Properties**:
  - _Outcome_: Bayesian analysis yields a posterior distribution, not a single value
  - _Spread_: Proportional to uncertainty
    - Decreases with more data
    - Decreases faster if data aligned with prior
    - With enough data, models with different priors converge to same result
  - _Order invariance_: Applying posterior sequentially or at once yields same result

::::
:::: {.column width=55%}

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L07.1.Updating_the_prior.png)

::::
:::

# Priors and Credible Intervals

## Frequentist vs Bayesian

* Do Priors Bias the Analysis?

- @Problem@: Detractors of the Bayesian approach complain that:
  - _"One should let the data speak!"_
  - The prior doesn't let the data speak for itself

- @Counterpoints@
  1. _"Data doesn't speak, but murmurs"_
     - Data doesn't have meaning per-se
     - Data can make sense only in context of models (e.g., mental models,
       mathematical models)
     - A prior is a mathematical model
  2. Every statistical model has a prior, even if not explicit
     - Frequentist statistics still makes assumptions (i.e., has a prior), but are
       hidden
     - E.g., maximum likelihood estimate (MLE) in frequentist approach corresponds to
       a uniform prior and choosing the mode of the posterior

* Advantages of Priors

- @Pros@
  - _Explicit assumptions_: Instead of hidden by frequentist or hacker ML approach
  - _Deeper understanding_
    - Encourages deeper analysis of problem and data
    - Forces understanding before seeing data
  - _Less overfitting_: The prior shrinks estimates toward plausible values,
    acting as a regularizer
  - _Uncertainty measure_: Spread of distribution measures uncertainty
  - _Faster inference_: Well-chosen prior simplifies and speeds up inference
    - _"When you encounter computational problems, there's often an issue with your
      model"_ (Gelman, 2008) `#cite("gelman2008folk")`{=typst}

* How to Choose Priors (1/2)

- @Variants@
  - _Weakly-informative priors_ (aka "flat", "vague", "diffuse priors")
    - Provide minimal information, e.g.,
      - Coefficient of linear regression centered around 0:
        $\beta \sim Normal(0, 10)$
  - _Regularizing priors_
    - Known information about the parameter, e.g.,
      - Parameter is positive: $\sigma \sim HalfCauchy(0, 5)$
        `#cite("gelman2006prior")`{=typst}
      - Parameter close to zero, above/below a number, or in a range
      - $\beta \sim Laplace(0, 1)$ (lasso prior) encourages sparsity
        `#cite("parkcasella2008lasso")`{=typst}
      - $\beta \sim Normal(0, 1)$ discourages extreme values

* How to Choose Priors (2/2)

- @Variants@
  - _Informative priors_
    - Strong priors from previous knowledge (expert opinion, studies), e.g.,
      - From experimental data: $\beta_1 \sim Normal(2.5, 0.5)$
      - From previous data, about 5% of cases positive: $p \sim Beta(2, 38)$
  - _Prior elicitation_
    - Compute least informative distribution given constraints, e.g.,
      - Estimate distribution using maximum entropy to satisfy constraints
      - E.g., beta distribution with 90% of mass between 0.1 and 0.7

## Communicating Results

* Communicating a Bayesian Analysis

::: columns
:::: {.column width=65%}

- @Procedure@
  1. **Communicate assumptions and hypothesis**
     - Describe priors and probabilistic models
     - E.g., coin-flip distributions:
       $$
       \begin{equation*}
         \begin{cases}
         \theta \sim \Beta(\alpha, \beta) \\
         y \sim Binomial(n=1, p=\theta) \\
         \end{cases}
       \end{equation*}
       $$
  2. **Communicate Bayesian analysis result**
     - Describe posterior distribution
     - Summarize location and dispersion
       - Mean (or mode, median)
       - Std dev (although misleading for skewed distributions)
     - Highest density interval (HDI) `#cite("kruschke2015doing")`{=typst}
       - Shortest interval containing a portion of probability density (e.g., 95% or
         50%)
       - Amount is arbitrary (e.g., `ArviZ` defaults to 94%)

::::
:::: {.column width=35%}

![Kruschke diagram of the coin model](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L07.1.Kruschke_diagram.png){height=50%}

::::
:::

* Confidence Intervals vs Credible Intervals

- @Remark@: People confuse _frequentist confidence intervals_ with _Bayesian credible
  intervals_ `#cite("morey2016fallacy")`{=typst}

- @Comparison@
  - **Frequentist**: In the frequentist framework, there is a true (unknown)
    parameter value
    - A _confidence interval_ may or may not contain the true parameter value
    - Interpretation of a 95% confidence interval
      - _No_: _"There is a 95% probability that the true value is in this interval"_
      - _Yes_: _"If repeated many times, 95% of intervals would contain the true
        value"_
  - **Bayesian**: In the Bayesian framework, parameters are random variables
    - Interpretation of a 95% _Bayesian credible interval_
      - _"There is a 95% probability that the true parameter lies within this
        interval, given the observed data"_
      - Bayesian _credible interval_ is intuitive

* Confidence vs Credible Intervals: Fishing Analogy

// TODO(ai_gp): Add an image somehow

- @Comparison@
  - **Confidence Interval (Frequentist)**:
    - Imagine fishing in a lake without seeing the fish
    - You throw your net
    - 95% confidence interval: _"If I threw this net 100 times, about 95 nets would
      catch the fish."_
    - Important: Once the net is thrown, it either caught the fish or not
      - The 95% makes sense across many attempts
  - **Credible Interval (Bayesian)**:
    - Imagine a magical map showing where fish _probably_ is, based on past
      observations
    - 95% credible interval: _"Given my map, there's a 95% chance the fish is inside
      this part of the lake."_
    - The fish's location is uncertain, and probability describes your belief

// 2, Programming probabilistically

# Probabilistic Programming

## Probabilistic Programming Languages

* Knowns and Unknowns

::: columns
:::: {.column width=65%}

- @Components@
  - The **"knowns"**
    - Model structure (modeled as a graph of probability distributions)
    - Data, observations (modeled as constants)
  - The **"unknowns"**
    - Model parameters (modeled as probability distributions)

- @Goal@: Use Bayes' theorem to:
  - Condition unknowns to knowns
  - Reduce the uncertainty about the unknowns

::::
:::: {.column width=35%}

```graphviz
digraph BayesTheorem {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=1.0;
    ranksep=0.75;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.4];

    // Node styles
    knowns       [label="Knowns", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    unknowns_in  [label="Unknowns", fillcolor="#A6E7F4"];
    bayes_box    [label="Bayes' Theorem", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    unknowns_out [label="Updated Unknowns", fillcolor="#B2E2B2"];

    // Force ranks
    { rank=same; knowns; unknowns_in }

    // Edges
    knowns      -> bayes_box;
    unknowns_in -> bayes_box;
    bayes_box   -> unknowns_out;
}
```

::::
:::

- @Problem@: Most probabilistic models are analytically intractable

- @Solution@: Probabilistic programming
  - Specify a probabilistic model using code
  - Solve models using numerical techniques

* Probabilistic Programming Languages

- @Components@
  - `PyMC`: Flexible Python library for probabilistic programming
    `#cite("abrilpla2023pymc")`{=typst}
  - `PyTensor`: Library to define, optimize, evaluate mathematical expressions
    using tensors, the computational backend of `PyMC`
  - `ArviZ`: Library to interpret probabilistic model results
    `#cite("kumar2019arviz")`{=typst}

- @Procedure@
  1. Specify models using code
  2. Numerical methods solve inference problems without need of user to
     understand how

- @Pros@
  - Compute results without analytical closed form
  - Treat model solving as a black box (e.g., universal inference engines)
  - Focus on model design, evaluation, interpretation

- @Remark@: Probabilistic programming languages
  - Have a similar impact as Fortran on scientific computing
  - Build algorithms but ignore computational details

## Coin Example: Numerical Solution

* Coin Example: Numerical Solution (1/3)

- @Example@
  - Assume you know the true value of $\theta$ (not true in general)
  - It's a synthetic example

- @Procedure@
  1. Model the prior $\theta$ and the likelihood $Y | \theta$
     $$
     \begin{equation*}
       \begin{cases}
       \theta \sim Beta(\alpha = 1, \beta = 1) \\
       Y \sim Binomial(n = 1, p = \theta) \\
       \end{cases}
     \end{equation*}
     $$
  2. Observe samples of the variable $Y$
  3. Run inference
  4. Generate samples of the posterior
  5. Summarize posterior
     - E.g., Highest Density Interval (HDI)

* Coin Example: Numerical Solution (2/3)

::: columns
:::: {.column width=45%}

- @Procedure@
  - Generate data from ground truth model
  - Build `PyMC` model matching mathematical model
  - `PyMC` uses NUTS sampler `#cite("hoffman2014nuts")`{=typst}, computes 4
    chains
  - Kernel density estimation (KDE) of the posterior looks like a Beta
  - Traces appear "noisy" and no trace diverges (good)
  - Numerical summary of posterior: Mean, std dev, HDI
    - $\EE[\hat{\theta}] \approx 0.324$
    - $\Pr(\hat{\theta} \in [0.031, 0.653]) = 0.94$

::::
:::: {.column width=52%}

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L07.1.Coin_example_numerical_solution.png)

::::
:::

* Coin Example: Numerical Solution (3/3)

::: columns
:::: {.column width=40%}

- @Procedure@
  1. Compute single KDE for all chains
  2. Rank plot to check results
     - Histograms should look uniform, exploring different (and all) posterior
       regions
  3. Plot single KDE with all statistics

